---
name: ptt-pr-draft-reply
description: "Brief the plan for answering a review of this branch's local request after the user's decision: approach per comment, resulting commits, and verification. Details on request. Writes nothing; /ptt-pr-reply carries it out and records the reply. Argument: none or the request number N."
disable-model-invocation: true
---

# Draft a reply to a review

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the records this skill
writes (it writes none); other work needs AtE (rules file).

The branch is the one the user names; otherwise the branch of the worktree
this holder's task works in (`worktrees/<topic>`; if several, list them and
ask). Run every git command from the workspace root with `git -C
<worktree>`; never ask the user to change directory. Request numbers may be
short (`4`, `pr-4`, `000004`). Without N, the request is this branch's one
open request whose latest review needs an answer; if there is none or
several, list them (`<records>/bin/pr list all --branch BRANCH`) and ask.
With N, the request
must be in `pr/open`: if `<records>/bin/pr list all` shows it merged or
closed, report its state and decision and stop. A merged, rejected, or
withdrawn request takes no draft or reply; a pending draft of it lapses.
A follow-up is a new request (`/ptt-pr-submit`).

1. Read `<records>/bin/pr show N`, the latest review and its decision, and
   the code on the branch. Follow the rules file's review timing: before
   the current review has a decision, give only the author's stance per
   comment and stop, without a change plan or execution question. A merge,
   reject, or withdraw decision ends the draft even if the request has
   not yet moved. Continue only after a decision requesting changes.
2. Brief the user on the plan, one line per item: per comment ID,
   agree or disagree and the approach (with the reason when
   disagreeing); the commit design after the change, one line per
   resulting commit, saying which existing commits are folded into,
   split, or added, and whether history is rewritten (recovery tag);
   the verification planned and what is Unknown. No diffs and no reply
   text unless the user asks; `ptt-pr-reply` writes the reply from the
   agreed plan and its results.
3. Write nothing: no file, no commit, no recorded message. End with: "To
   carry out this plan, run `/ptt-pr-reply NNNNNN`; say first what to
   change, if anything."
